# {{PROJECT}}

## Spec-driven development (Fleet SDD)

This repo uses Fleet SDD. Work is defined in artifacts under `{{SPECS_DIR}}/`
and gated by a sign-off ledger. Both humans and agents follow the same rules —
that is the point.

### The rule

**No production code without an approved spec.** For anything beyond tier
`tiny`, an approved `design.md` too. If you are about to write code and cannot
name the feature directory it belongs to, stop and ask.

Prototypes and spikes are exempt, and spike code does not ship.

### Where to start

```bash
npx fleet-sdd next
```

That prints the active stage, the artifact it produces, which gates are
blocking, and which role owns each. It is the authority on what to do next —
more reliable than reading the repo and guessing.

In Claude Code, `/sdd:next` does the same and then loads the right role.

### Three concepts

**Artifacts** are the shared truth, in `{{SPECS_DIR}}/<nnn-slug>/`:

| File | Holds | Owned by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `spec.md` | Problem, users, scope, acceptance criteria, UX | Product, UX |
| `design.md` | Approach, data, interfaces, security, performance, observability | Architect, Tech Lead, Security, Performance, Observability |
| `tasks.md` | Task table, traceability, test approach | Product Owner, Tech Lead, Test Lead |
| `evidence.md` | What was built and what was proven | Developer, QA, Test Lead, DevOps |
| `runbook.md` | Alerts, deploy and rollback, support triage | Observability, DevOps, Support |
| `gates.yml` | Who signed off on what, and when | everyone |

**Roles** are lenses on those files, in `.sdd/roles/*.md`. Thirteen ship by
default. Each declares the sections it `owns` and the `gates` it may sign.

**Flows** are ordered stages with gates, in `.sdd/flows/*.md`: `feature`,
`bugfix`, `spike`.

### Rules for agents working here

1. **Read the role file before writing.** `.sdd/roles/<role>.md` says what that
   role does, what it must never sign off on, and which sections it owns. Check
   `.sdd/overrides/` first — an override shadows the shipped file.
2. **Stay in your section.** Several roles share each artifact. Writing into
   another role's section dissolves the ownership the framework depends on. If
   another role's section blocks you, say so.
3. **Never approve a gate.** A gate is a person accepting accountability. Do the
   work, report it, and offer the command. `fleet-sdd gate <id> approve` is for
   humans.
4. **Leave `TODO(sdd)` where you do not know.** An honest gap is useful; invented
   content that reads as settled is not. `check` fails if a placeholder survives
   sign-off, which is deliberate.
5. **Read `.sdd/constitution.md` first.** A principle there outranks convenience
   and any deadline.
6. **Run `npx fleet-sdd check` before you claim to be finished.** Exit 1 means
   errors.

### Commands

```bash
npx fleet-sdd next                       # what is next and who owns it
npx fleet-sdd new "<title>"              # scaffold a feature
npx fleet-sdd status                     # every feature, stage, blockers
npx fleet-sdd check                      # validate artifacts and gates
npx fleet-sdd roles [id]                 # list roles, or print one
npx fleet-sdd gate <id> <verdict> -m ""  # record a sign-off (humans)
```

### Extending

Add a role by dropping a Markdown file in `.sdd/roles/`. No registration, no
code change. See `.sdd/EXTENDING.md`.
